        <div class="section-title">General Excitability & Fiber Properties</div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">1. Which of the following best defines the concept of physiological "Excitability"?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A.</strong> The constant passive movement of ions down their chemical gradients.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B.</strong> The ability of a living tissue to respond to an adequate stimulus and produce an action potential.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C.</strong> The physical transformation of cellular proteins during temperature alterations.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D.</strong> The active extrusion of metabolic waste particles across the sarcolemma.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">2. According to the lecture materials, which tissues in the human body exhibit the highest degree of excitability?[span_2](start_span)[span_2](end_span)</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A.</strong> Nerve and muscle cells.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B.</strong> Bone microenvironment and cartilage matrices.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C.</strong> Epithelial and connective tissues.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D.</strong> Hepatic parenchymal and adipose tissues.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">3. Which group of nerve fibers possesses a thick myelin sheath, displays diameters between 2-20 µm, and propagates signals at velocities of 10-120 m/sec?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A.</strong> Type C fibers.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B.</strong> Type B fibers.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C.</strong> Unmyelinated postganglionic fibers.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D.</strong> Type A fibers.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">4. A clinician applies prolonged, deep continuous pressure over a peripheral nerve group. Which class of nerve fibers is characteristically the most sensitive to this mechanical disruption?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A.</strong> Small unmyelinated autonomic paths.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B.</strong> Type B fibers.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C.</strong> Type A fibers.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D.</strong> Type C fibers.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">5. During localized tissue hypoxia or a prolonged lack of Oxygen (O₂ lack), which class of nerve fibers undergoes block or functional impairment first?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A.</strong> Type A fibers.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B.</strong> Type B fibers.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C.</strong> Type C fibers.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D.</strong> Thick myelinated motor pathways.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">6. Why are Type C fibers described as highly sensitive to local anesthetics compared to other fiber categories?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A.</strong> Because they are thin unmyelinated fibers with small diameters (0.5-1 µm).</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B.</strong> Because they possess a very large diameter (20 µm) that concentrates the drug.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C.</strong> Because they are heavily myelinated with multiple nodes of Ranvier.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D.</strong> Because they lack voltage-gated sodium channels entirely.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
