                <div class="option-item"><strong>D.</strong> It loses its myelin sheath, and its neurilemma becomes continuous with the sarcolemma.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">9. The terminal button (synaptic knob) contains numerous organelles. What is the specific functional purpose of the multiple mitochondria located there?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A.</strong> They actively degrade and clear used neurotransmitters from the gutter.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B.</strong> They provide the metabolic energy required for the synthesis of acetylcholine (ACh).</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C.</strong> They synthesize calcium ions inside the pre-synaptic pool.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D.</strong> They act as voltage sensors for the propagating action potential.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">10. Which specific ion channels present on the pre-synaptic axolemma open in response to the arriving nerve action potential, triggering exocytosis?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A.</strong> Voltage-gated Ca2+ channels.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B.</strong> Voltage-gated K+ channels.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C.</strong> Voltage-gated Na+ channels.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D.</strong> Acetylcholine-gated cation channels.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">11. Where is the acetylcholinesterase (AChE) enzyme precisely situated within the neuromuscular junction infrastructure?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A.</strong> Inside the synaptic vesicles of the terminal button.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B.</strong> Anchored purely on the pre-synaptic release sites.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C.</strong> Within the synaptic cleft space.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D.</strong> Inside the longitudinal sarcoplasmic tubules.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">12. What specific receptor type is found clustered at the post-synaptic invaginations (synaptic gutter) of the sarcolemma?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A.</strong> Muscarinic cholinergic receptors.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B.</strong> Nicotinic receptors (Acetylcholine-gated ion channels).</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C.</strong> Alpha-1 adrenergic receptors.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D.</strong> Ryanodine receptors.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">13. Which of the following correctly describes the properties of normal neuromuscular transmission?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A.</strong> Unidirectional flow, 0.5 milliseconds delay, fatigable due to depletion of ACh.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B.</strong> Bidirectional flow, 5.0 milliseconds delay, highly resistant to fatigue.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C.</strong> Unidirectional flow, zero time delay, completely obeys the all-or-none law at the EPP level.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D.</strong> Multidirectional electrical coupling bypassing chemical intermediaries.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">14. What occurs when the generated End Plate Potential (EPP) successfully reaches the firing level?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A.</strong> It immediately activates acetylcholinesterase synthesis.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B.</strong> It reverses the direction of transmission back to the axon trunk.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C.</strong> It induces immediate rigor mortis by locking myosin heads.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D.</strong> It spreads to both sides of the motor end plate, initiating a propagating muscle action potential.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

